course: books
lesson: 40
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter IV, part 4: Mr. Badger (the city under the Wild Wood, and the way home)
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: While the Rat and the Otter argue about eels by the fire, the Badger takes the Mole on a tour with a lantern. There are passages and rooms everywhere, pillars, arches and stone floors. «How did you ever build all this?» asks the Mole. «I didn't,» says the Badger.
explain: Long ago, he explains, there was a city of people here — rich, powerful builders who thought their city would last for ever. They went away; wind and rain and growing trees buried it, and the badgers moved back in. «People come… and they go. But we remain.» The Wild Wood grew on top, with all sorts of animals, good, bad and so-so.
explain: The Badger promises to pass the word around: any friend of his can walk where he likes. Back in the kitchen the Rat is restless, overcoat on and pistols in his belt, worried the river will run off without him. So the Badger leads them along a long secret tunnel to the edge of the wood and hides the opening behind them.
explain: They hurry home across the snowy fields towards the river. Looking back at the dark Wild Wood, the Mole understands himself at last: he is an animal of fields and hedgerows and gardens, and that quiet life holds adventure enough for him.
fact: The Rat and the Otter start «a heated argument on the subject of eels» in the chimney-corner; the Badger lights a lantern for the Mole.
fact: The Mole is «staggered at the size, the extent, the ramifications of it all» (ramifications = branches, more and more passages leading off) — passages, store-chambers, «pillars, the arches, the pavements».
fact: Some rooms are «mere cupboards, others nearly as broad and imposing as Toad's dining-hall» (imposing = big and grand).
fact: The Badger: «It would be astonishing indeed, if I had done it. But as a matter of fact I did none of it—only cleaned out the passages and chambers, as far as I had need of them.»
fact: «Very long ago, on the spot where the Wild Wood waves now … there was a city—a city of people, you know … They built to last, for they thought their city would last for ever.»
fact: «People come—they stay for a while, they flourish, they build—and they go. It is their way. But we remain.» (Flourish = do very well.) Badgers were there before the city, «and now there are badgers here again».
fact: First it was «all down, down, down, gradually—ruin and levelling and disappearance», then «all up, up, up», as seeds grew to saplings and saplings to trees, and leaf-mould and sand covered the stones.
fact: The animals above «don't bother themselves about the past» or about «the future when perhaps the people will move in again». The wood holds «good, bad, and indifferent—I name no names. It takes all sorts to make a world.»
fact: The Badger: «we must all live and let live» (let others live their way). «Any friend of mine walks where he likes in this country, or I'll know the reason why!»
fact: The Rat is «very restless»: the underground air gets on his nerves and he seems «afraid that the river would run away if he wasn't there to look after it».
fact: The Otter: «if there's a head that needs to be punched, you can confidently rely upon me to punch it.»
fact: The Badger has «bolt-holes to the edge of the wood in several directions» (bolt-hole = secret escape tunnel), «which I don't want everybody to know about». The tunnel is «part vaulted, part hewn through solid rock» and seems «miles» long; at the end he hides the mouth with «creepers, brushwood, and dead leaves».
fact: Far ahead they saw «a glint of the familiar old river». The Otter, who knew all the paths, took charge of the party and led them home.
fact: From a stile they look back at the wood, «dense, menacing, compact», and hurry home to the river «that never made them afraid with any amazement».
fact: The Mole sees «that he was an animal of tilled field and hedge-row» (tilled = ploughed for crops); he «must keep to the pleasant places … which held adventure enough … to last for a lifetime».
fact: Talk in this lesson: «gurt» (West Country, England) = great, big; «proper job» (West Country) = well done; «grand» (Ireland) = fine; «yinz» (Pittsburgh, US) = you all; «delulu» = dreaming, not facing reality.
exercise: What do the Rat and the Otter argue about while the others explore? | Eels | Whose boat is the fastest on the whole river | Toad's cars | The weather
exercise: What does the Badger carry on the tour? | A lantern | A candle and a big bunch of keys | A map of the wood | A cudgel and his pistols
exercise: Which of these did the Mole NOT see on the tour? | A river running through the rooms | Pillars and arches | Crammed store-chambers | Long dim passages
exercise: Some rooms were nearly as big as which room we have heard of before? | Toad's dining-hall | The Rat's little parlour beside the river | Badger's warm kitchen | Mole's little front hall
exercise: «How on earth did you ever find time and strength to do all this?» What does the Badger answer? | He did none of it — he only cleaned it out | He built it all himself over a hundred years, stone by stone | The hedgehogs built it for him | The weasels built it
exercise: What stood on that spot, very long ago? | A city of people | A huge castle full of knights and soldiers | A great lake full of fish | A forest of giant trees
exercise: What kind of people were they, says the Badger? | Powerful, rich, great builders | Poor and lazy farmers who never built anything | Small and shy | Cruel and wicked
exercise: Why did they build so strongly? | They thought their city would last for ever | They were afraid of the badgers and wanted thick walls | They had too many stones | The king ordered it
exercise: What happened to those people? | Nobody can tell — they went | They were all chased away by the badgers in a great war | They turned into animals | They live under the river
exercise: «People come … and they go. But we remain.» Who are «we»? | The badgers | All the animals of the Wild Wood, together | The Rat and the Mole | The trees
exercise: Who was there first, before the city? | Badgers | Weasels and stoats, in their holes | People who came later | Nobody, only trees
exercise: What buried the old city? | Wind, rain, growing trees, leaf-mould and soil | A great flood, all in one night | An earthquake | The people themselves
exercise: What do the animals living above think about the past? | They don't bother about it | They worry about it every day and tell stories of it | They are afraid of it | They write it down
exercise: Who does the Badger say lives in the Wild Wood now? | All sorts — good, bad and indifferent | Only wicked weasels and stoats | Only rabbits | Nobody but badgers
exercise: «It takes all sorts to make a world.» What does this mean? | The world is made of all kinds of people, and that's how it is | Only good animals should be allowed to live in the world, and bad ones sent far away | The world needs more badgers | Everyone should be the same
exercise: What does the Badger promise the Mole? | No more trouble — any friend of his walks where he likes | A big present of honey and apples and nuts to take home to the river bank | A room of his own | A pistol
exercise: Why is the Rat so restless when they return? | The underground air gets on his nerves, and he misses the river | He has lost his argument about eels with the Otter and is in a terrible sulk | He is hungry | He is afraid of the Badger
exercise: What has the Rat put on, ready to go? | His overcoat, with pistols in his belt | His goloshes and his best cap | A dressing-gown | Nothing
exercise: What does the Otter offer to do on the way home? | Punch any head that needs punching | Carry the Mole on his back all the way | Sing rowing songs to keep them cheerful | Row them all home in his own little boat
exercise: How does the Badger get them out of the wood? | Along a secret tunnel to its edge | He walks them through the wood in the daylight | By boat | He calls a cart
exercise: What is a «bolt-hole»? | A secret escape tunnel | A hole for the bolt of a door | A rabbit's bedroom | A hole for keeping arrows
exercise: What does the Badger do after pushing them out of the tunnel? | Hides the opening with creepers, brushwood and leaves | Waves goodbye and walks home with them | Locks a door | Shouts for the hedgehogs
exercise: What do they see far ahead when they come out? | A glint of the familiar old river | Toad Hall, with every window lit up | The ruins of the old city of people | The Mole's home, Mole End, far away
exercise: Who leads the party home, and why? | The Otter, who knows all the paths | The Mole, because his nose is the best | The Badger, because it's his own wood | The Rat, as usual, with his pistols
exercise: What does the Mole decide he is, as he hurries home? | An animal of tilled field and hedgerow | A brave animal of the deep Wild Wood, like the Badger | A water animal, like the Rat | A traveller of the Wide World
test: Ruby, delulu as usual: «Badger dug the whole gurt place by himself, proper job.» (gurt = great; delulu = not facing facts) Put it right. | He did none of it — people built it, he only cleaned it | Right: he dug every passage himself, over a very long time | The hedgehogs dug it | The Mole dug it
test: Is this TRUE or FALSE? «The Badger says the people will never, ever come back.» | False: he says perhaps they will, for a time | True: he says they are gone for ever and ever | True, the badgers chased them | False: he says they are coming back tomorrow
test: «They're not so bad really; and we must all live and let live.» What is the Badger asking the Mole to do? | Let the Wild Wood animals live their own way, without hating them | Go and fight the weasels and stoats with the Otter tomorrow morning at sunrise | Move into the Wild Wood | Forget about Toad
test: Callum, bit scared: «so them weasels'll get Mole again next time?» What did Badger promise? | He'll pass the word that his friends walk where they like | He'll fight every weasel in the wood himself, one by one, with his big stick | He'll build a wall | Nothing
test: Why does the Badger not want everybody to know about his bolt-holes? | Secret ways out are only safe if they stay secret | He is ashamed of them because they are dirty, damp and full of spiders | They are too small | They belong to the Otter
test: Looking back: in chapter III, what was «the Terror of the Wild Wood» for the Mole? | Faces at holes, then whistling, then pattering feet closing in | A great storm with thunder and lightning that shook all the trees and broke them | A giant badger | A ghost of the old city
test: Looking back: in chapter I, what did the Rat say lies beyond the Wild Wood? | The Wide World — «something that doesn't matter» | Toad Hall and its big gardens | A great sea | The old city of people
test: Looking back: where did the Mole hide in chapter III when he was too tired to run? | In the hollow of an old beech tree | In a rabbit's burrow under a stump | Up a tall fir tree | In the Badger's coal-cellar
test: Looking back: in chapter I, how did the Rat describe the river? | «It's my world, and I don't want any other» | «A dull place, with nothing to do in the winter» | «Dangerous, and full of pike» | «Just water»
test: Looking back: the Rat seems «afraid that the river would run away». In chapter I, the river was compared to which creature? | A sleek, sinuous, full-bodied animal, chasing and chuckling | A sleepy old dog lying in the sun all day long, never moving at all | A bird | A fish
test: The Mole began the book bursting out of his hole into the sunshine. How has he changed by the end of chapter IV? | He knows now he belongs to fields, hedges and the river — not the wild | He wants to go back underground for good and never leave his hole again | He wants to travel the Wide World | He has not changed
test: What would happen if the Badger's tunnels were known to everyone? | Enemies could use them to get into his home | Nothing at all — tunnels can't be used by anyone | The Rat would be pleased | The city would come back
test: Jayden from Pittsburgh: «yinz see the Wild Wood from the stile? it looks all friendly now.» (yinz = you all) Put it right. | It looks dense, menacing and grim against the white snow | Right: it looks warm and welcoming in the sunshine | They can't see it from there | It has been cut down
test: «Grand,» says Niamh from Cork, «so the Mole will want more adventures in the Wild Wood now.» (grand = fine) Put it right. | He decides to keep to the pleasant places, which hold adventure enough | Right: he plans to go back alone next week to explore the deepest parts of the wood | He wants to live with the Badger | He wants to fight the weasels
test: ★★★ «All down, down, down … Then it was all up, up, up.» What went DOWN, and what went UP? | The city went down; the forest grew up | The badgers went down; the people went up | The river went down; the snow went up | The sun went down; the moon came up
test: ★★★ Careful: who went on the tour of the old city's rooms after luncheon, while others argued about eels? | Only the Mole, with the Badger | All three animals together | Only the Rat, alone | Only the Otter and the Rat, by the fire
test: ★★★ Count the different animals (kinds, not each one) we meet inside Badger's house in chapter IV: badger, rat, mole… and? | Otter and hedgehog — five kinds | Only the otter — four kinds | Rabbit, otter and hedgehog — six kinds | No more — three kinds
exam: Identify the origin of the passages and chambers inhabited by the Badger. | The remains of a city of people, long since vanished | Excavation by the Badger himself over the course of his lifetime | Excavation by the weasels | A natural cavern
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Badger's view of the relation between people and badgers? | People come and go, whereas badgers endure and return | People are permanent, whereas badgers are transient and soon forgotten | The two cannot coexist | Badgers built the city
exam: It is given that the Rat desired to depart. Ascertain the means of departure provided by the Badger. | A tunnel to the edge of the wood | A conducted walk across the wood by daylight | Transport by boat upon the river | None
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the Mole's conclusion upon the homeward journey. | That he belonged to the cultivated country of field and hedgerow | That he was by nature suited to the hardships of the Wild Wood | That he should travel | That he should live underground with the Badger
